EME permission -- COMMON half (LW-M1-03).

Split out of patches/eme-permission.patch, which was a straddler: one file in
core Gecko (the enforcement point) plus two browser/-only files (the desktop
doorhanger). The browser/ half now lives in patches/eme-permission-desktop.patch;
common + desktop together are byte-for-byte the original patch.

What is here:
  dom/media/eme/MediaKeySystemAccessManager.cpp   core DOM/media, built on every
                                                  target (dom/media/moz.build:28,32
                                                  puts "eme" in DIRS, no guard)

What it does, in the order the code runs:
  1. RequestMediaKeySystemAccess(): drops upstream's Clear Key exemption from the
     media.eme.enabled gate, so media.eme.enabled=false now blocks Clear Key too.
  2. RequestMediaKeySystemAccess(): rejects Clear Key outright unless
     librewolf.eme.gmp-clearkey.enabled is true. Read with
     Preferences::GetBool(..., false) -- the fallback when the pref is absent is
     DENY.
  3. OnDoesAppAllowProtectedMedia(): rejection-message wording only, no behaviour
     change.

ANDROID BEHAVIOUR -- THE DECISION, AND THE TREE EVIDENCE FOR IT
===============================================================
Question raised by LW-M1-01: with the prompt UI in the other half, does the
Android path still reach a decision, or does EME fail closed with no UI, the way
landmine L1 kills WebGL?

Answer: it reaches a real decision, and Android already has real UI for it. This
is NOT L1's shape, and NO Android companion patch is needed. All of the below was
checked against the pristine firefox-153.0.4 tree.

  * The "media-key-system-access" request is the app-approval request created in
    MediaKeySystemAccessManager::CheckDoesAppAllowProtectedMedia() (:247), gated
    on media.eme.require-app-approval
    (MediaKeySystemAccessPermissionRequest.cpp:41). That pref defaults to false
    (StaticPrefList.yaml:12193-12196); LibreWolf turns it on for desktop in
    settings/librewolf.cfg:397, and upstream already turns it on for Android in
    mobile/android/app/geckoview-prefs.js:287. Both front ends really do run the
    prompt path.
  * On Android the prompt is answered, not dropped. The
    @mozilla.org/content-permission/prompt;1 contract is bound to
    GeckoViewPermission (mobile/shared/components/geckoview/components.conf:28-33),
    whose promptPermission() is type-agnostic: it forwards ANY perm.type to the
    embedder through the GeckoViewPermission actor
    (GeckoViewPermission.sys.mjs:119-219, the actor call at :169). There is no
    per-type switch to fall off. That is exactly the difference from
    webgl-permission, whose prompt observers exist only under browser/.
  * The type survives the JNI boundary: GeckoSession.java:7354-7355 maps
    "media-key-system-access" to PERMISSION_MEDIA_KEY_SYSTEM_ACCESS (:7195), and
    :7386-7387 maps it back.
  * Fenix implements it end to end, with a visible prompt and a settings entry:
    PhoneFeature.MEDIA_KEY_SYSTEM_ACCESS
    (fenix/app/.../settings/PhoneFeature.kt:38), fed into the site-permission
    rules at fenix/app/.../utils/Settings.kt:1812 and answered by
    SitePermissionsRules.kt:92-94. The default action is ASK_TO_ALLOW
    (Settings.kt:1749-1753), i.e. the user is prompted, and Fenix exposes both a
    global and a per-site Allowed/Blocked control for it.
  * Consequence: on Android, Widevine (media.eme.enabled defaults TRUE there --
    StaticPrefList.yaml:12163-12176 puts only non-Android Linux in the false
    branch) reaches GetKeySystemStatus, then the Fenix doorhanger, then the user.

What IS a silent deny, deliberately, and on BOTH platforms: Clear Key. Hunk 2
rejects it with "CDM is not installed" before any prompt is reached, because the
enforcement sits in RequestMediaKeySystemAccess() while
CheckDoesAppAllowProtectedMedia() only runs later, after GetSupportedConfig()
(MediaKeySystemAccessManager.cpp:532-542). That is the existing desktop LibreWolf
behaviour, unchanged here; it is not an Android-specific regression.

Why that needs no Android default flip (the contrast with landmine L1): the pref
is read as Preferences::GetBool("librewolf.eme.gmp-clearkey.enabled", false). The
built-in fallback is already the strict value, and LibreWolf's own default
(settings/librewolf.cfg:395) is the same false. So an Android build that has not
yet grown librewolf.cfg behaves identically to desktop. webgl-permission needed
patches/android/webgl-prompt-default.patch precisely because its shared default
was the permissive-looking true, which on Android meant "prompt", which meant
"never answered".

One upstream asymmetry, present before and after this patch, recorded so nobody
rediscovers it as a regression: the "mediakeys-request" notification sent by
MediaKeySystemAccess::NotifyObservers() is observed only by
browser/actors/EncryptedMediaChild.sys.mjs (registered at
browser/components/DesktopActorRegistry.sys.mjs:488). Nothing under mobile/
observes it, so the Api_disabled / Cdm_not_installed "chrome should explain this"
notifications are no-ops on Android. Hunk 1 makes Clear Key reach that
notification where upstream did not; on Android that is a notification into the
void -- harmless, and the user-visible deny reason is the DOMException the promise
is rejected with.

Deferred to M2+ (do not build it here, per the task brief): an Android surface for
the LibreWolf-specific Clear Key denial, the equivalent of the desktop
EMEPermissionPrompt's learn-more link to librewolf.eme.warning.infoURL.

